            <ArticleTitle>Paravertebral cervical nerve block in a patient suffering from a Pancoast tumor.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1799-802</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1526-4637.2010.00994.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In patients with aggressive tumors resistant to conventional pain treatment, regional anaesthesia frequently becomes an alternative therapy. Cervical paravertebral nerve block among several access options to the brachial plexus is barely ever used. We present a case with severe shoulder and upper extremity pain owing to an expanding Pancoast tumor exhibiting compression upon the brachial plexus. Continuous intrathecal morphine infusion and adjuvant treatment was not sufficient to render the patient pain-free. With the addition of paravertebral nerve blockade the patient's pain improved substantially, however without impacting his longevity.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
